        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 也",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "也 — yě — \"also; too\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我也要 wǒ yě yào — Me too; I'd like one too."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "他也是学生 tā yě shì xuésheng — He's a student too."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The next lesson puts the character in your hand. You have written it before, as the right half of 他."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "也 (yě) is inherited from Middle Chinese yaeX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'a particle that closes a sentence'. 'Also' is not given there, so how it came to mean 'also' is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "也 goes in front of the verb: 我也要 (wǒ yě yào), 他也是学生. English puts too at the end; Mandarin does not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
